Stop memorizing operator names. Follow what happens to each value instead: once you can read one
value through a pipe, every operator is just a rule about what it does to that value.

## One result, or a stream of values

A **Promise** settles once: one fulfillment value or one rejection, at most. `new Promise(executor)`
runs the executor immediately, and its `.then()` callbacks always run asynchronously. It has no
built-in cancellation method.

An **Observable** delivers zero to many values, and may complete or error. A cold source starts a
new execution for each subscription; a hot source can share a producer that is already running.
Emissions can be synchronous or asynchronous.

`unsubscribe()` stops your subscription, not necessarily the source.

| | Promise | Observable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Values | One, or a rejection | Zero to many |
| Starts | The executor runs at once, in `new Promise()` | On subscribe (cold) or already running (hot) |
| Timing | Callbacks always asynchronous | Synchronous or asynchronous |
| Cancel | No built-in method | `unsubscribe()` stops your subscription |

## Reading the diagram

Time moves from left to right. Each dot is a value; each line is a stream.

{% diagram id="rxjs-fundamentals/reading" alt="A stream diagram: the values A and B on a line that runs from earlier to later, then a vertical bar for completion." caption="Two values, then completion." /%}

- `next(A)`: a value is emitted.
- `|` complete: the stream ends normally.
- `X` error: the stream ends with a failure.

No more values arrive after complete or error. These lines are not execution threads: they only
show when values happen.

## {% highlight %}pipe(){% /highlight %} composes, {% highlight %}subscribe(){% /highlight %} connects

```ts
const doubled$ = source$.pipe(map((n) => n * 2));

doubled$.subscribe(console.log);
```

{% highlight %}`pipe()`{% /highlight %} composes the operators into a new Observable without subscribing to the chain; a hot source may already be producing values on its own. {% highlight %}`subscribe()`{% /highlight %} registers the consumer and, on a cold source, starts its execution for that subscription. With Angular's `HttpClient`, each subscription sends a request. Angular's `async` pipe can manage the subscription for you.

## {% highlight %}map{% /highlight %} changes the value, {% highlight %}filter{% /highlight %} decides what passes

{% diagram id="rxjs-fundamentals/map" alt="Marble diagram: map(n => n * 10) turns 1, 2 and 3 into 10, 20 and 30." caption="map changes every value." /%}

{% diagram id="rxjs-fundamentals/filter" alt="Marble diagram: filter(n => n >= 20) receives 10, 20 and 30 and lets only 20 and 30 through." caption="filter drops the values that fail the test." /%}

`filter` drops emissions; it does not emit `true` or `false`.

## {% highlight %}tap{% /highlight %} observes without transforming

{% diagram id="rxjs-fundamentals/tap" alt="Marble diagram: 20 and 30 enter tap, the console logs 20 and then 30, and the same 20 and 30 come out unchanged." caption="tap logs each value; the stream carries on unchanged." /%}

Returning a value from `tap` does not change the emitted value. Use it to observe; avoid mutating
objects inside it.

## Putting it together

Follow one value and read the whole pipe:

```ts
source$
  .pipe(
    map((n) => n * 10),
    filter((n) => n >= 20),
    tap((n) => console.log(n)),
  )
  .subscribe(render);
```

| Input | After map | Output |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1, 2, 3 | 10, 20, 30 | 20, 30 |

`2` becomes `20`. It passes the filter. `tap` logs `20`, and the subscriber receives `20`.

{% highlight %}map{% /highlight %} transforms, {% highlight %}filter{% /highlight %} selects, {% highlight %}tap{% /highlight %} observes.

{% quiz %}
{% question text="How many requests does this code send?" options=["None: there is no `pipe()`", "One, shared by both subscribers", "Two: one per subscription"] answer=3 explanation="`HttpClient` returns a cold Observable: each `subscribe()` starts its own execution, and each execution sends a request." %}
```ts
const user$ = this.http.get<User>('/api/user');
user$.subscribe(renderHeader);
user$.subscribe(renderMenu);
```
{% /question %}

{% question text="You call `pipe()` on a cold source but never `subscribe()`. What runs?" options=["The source, with its result cached for later", "Nothing: `pipe()` only composes a new Observable", "The operators, without the source"] answer=2 explanation="`pipe()` composes the operators without subscribing to the chain. On a cold source, nothing starts until `subscribe()`." /%}

{% question text="What does `render` receive?" options=["`false`, `true`, `true`", "`20`, `30`", "`10`, `20`, `30`"] answer=2 explanation="`filter` decides what passes: it drops `10` and emits `20` and `30` unchanged. It never emits `true` or `false`." %}
```ts
of(10, 20, 30).pipe(filter((n) => n >= 20)).subscribe(render);
```
{% /question %}

{% question text="What is logged?" options=["`10`", "`5`", "Nothing"] answer=2 explanation="Returning a value from `tap` does not change the emitted value: `tap` observes, `map` transforms." %}
```ts
of(5).pipe(tap((n) => n * 2)).subscribe(console.log);
```
{% /question %}

{% question text="A stream has emitted complete (`|`). What can arrive after it?" options=["More values, if the source keeps producing", "Nothing: no values arrive after complete or error", "Only an error"] answer=2 explanation="Complete and error both end the stream. No more values arrive after either." /%}
{% /quiz %}

Sources: [Observable](https://rxjs.dev/guide/observable) and [operators](https://rxjs.dev/guide/operators) in the RxJS docs, and the [HTTP client guide](https://angular.dev/guide/http) in Angular's docs.
